-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub — this repository's task graph (lib/task-graph.cub; its meaning and rules
-- are HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4). The product, Avik's four goals and the one dependency between them
-- (2026-11-13). A goal with no children is one coarse task described by its statement; it is split
-- only when part of it is being tackled immediately (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4). Known defects
-- and gaps found in the code are dated `finding (date):` comments at their sites.
--
import "../lib/task-graph.cub"
-- the-product (2026-11-13, Avik): "the product is every screened device in the world simply runs
-- hyperactive kernel. the term "application" becomes fully concrete - does not refer to a silo, but
-- literally to a particular application of computing." "*all* of this is to serve *product*." The
-- root: what Avik wants (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.1).
the-product : Node
the-product = node "The product: every screened device in the world runs Hyperactive." nil
-- language-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "complete implementation of hyperactive programming
-- language & features as specified by the paper ... get to the point i can show someone the paper
-- and correctly state "i have implemented this fully correctly" (obviously, that doesn't include
-- constructions about random examples about trading systems or message protocols)". The
-- initial high-priority deliverable.
-- What is known about this goal follows (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): facts,
-- not a breakdown and not parts; the session working on the goal decides how to break it down when its
-- work begins (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The implementation is shown to be
-- exactly the published calculus, rule by rule, and every deviation is fixed or justified. nobody
-- has compared the implementation with the published calculus, so whether it is "implemented fully
-- correctly" is unknown. Read the evaluator's rule cases (src/net.c), the translation of terms into
-- its graph (src/lang.c), the composition code generated per data type as unchecked text
-- (src/lang.c), universe composition (lib/prelude.cub, %hcompU, Cubical Agda's design, not CCHM's
-- Glue) and the checker's typing clauses (lib/check.cub) against CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber,
-- Mörtberg) and the clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi), recording each rule as
-- matching or deviating, then fix or justify each deviation. What is known follows. The deliverable
-- is not an agent's statement that the code is right: it is the trusted part made small enough to
-- be plainly the published rules, each rule case shown beside the rule it implements, everything
-- else checked or unable to change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work. For every data declaration this generates, as
-- untyped source text, the type's composition (%comp-D), its homogeneous composition, and transport
-- on each constructor and on formal compositions. That text is never checked: it is trusted as
-- CCHM's rules for the declared type, written once in C per declaration rather than as the single
-- schematic rule CCHM gives for inductive and higher inductive types. Show it is exactly that rule,
-- or replace the per-declaration generation by the schematic rule.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work. The calculus's rules are the cases of this function
-- (application, projections, path application, composition, Glue, interval and faces, systems,
-- higher-constructor boundaries, hcomp, transport, case: about 650 lines), the face algebra
-- (src/dnf.c), the composition clauses for the type formers (lib/prelude.cub) and the composition
-- code generated per data type (generated in src/lang.c). But the rule bodies also make
-- sharing, deferral and memoization decisions inline (V_DEFER, boxes and references, A8 free-
-- dimension cuts), and what a value is depends on the lazy-copy and substitution machinery above
-- being right. So the part that must be trusted is in effect this whole file. To make the
-- implementation's conformance to the published rules checkable by reading: the rules alone
-- determine results, and the machinery is confined to scheduling, sharing and identity, where by
-- construction (strong confluence of interaction nets; memo keys that are the full content of a
-- deterministic computation) it cannot change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/prelude.cub): needs-work. CCHM §7.1 defines composition in the universe
-- as a Glue type along the equivalences given by transport (comp^i U [φ ↦ E] A0 = Glue [φ ↦ (E(i1),
-- equiv_i E(1−i))] A0). Here it is instead its own type former, %hcompU, with unglue on a face as
-- transport back along that face's line (%unglue-line) and its own composition (%comp-gluel,
-- %tproof): the design of Cubical Agda (Vezzosi, Mörtberg, Abel). The extra face ¬φ ↦ A0 is
-- admissible (on ¬φ the tube is empty and the composite is A0; φ ∧ ¬φ is 0 in the face lattice),
-- and makes a closed composition of types compute to a type. Not yet read against Cubical Agda's
-- published rules for this type former, its unglue and its composition; until then these four
-- clauses are trusted without a reference reading.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading the
-- evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side
-- with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the
-- clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as
-- matching or deviating at its site. The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until
-- this is done, conformance of the implementation to the calculus is not known.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's
-- translation of terms into the evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning
-- (the strictness analysis that decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting,
-- sweeping of compiled code, the module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and
-- whose hits are trusted).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading
-- lib/check.cub's typing clauses against the calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or
-- deviating at its site.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The language's terms are typed
-- objects in it, with normalization and its soundness, and the checker returns typing derivations
-- (paper §6.1). paper §6.1. The language's terms as typed objects in it, normalization by
-- evaluation with its soundness, and a checker that returns typing derivations (lib/check.cub is an
-- untyped term with no statement of what it accepts; lib/std.cub's primitives give typed programs
-- its operations at asserted types). In progress in experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub; what remains is in
-- lib/nbe.cub's head (F2d, F2d', F2e, fund, normalize, the swap), then Kan operations, Glue,
-- universes and object-level data types. This is the easiest language to build it in, not a hard
-- one (2026-11-13, worked out with Avik): it is normalization for a cubical type theory written
-- inside a cubical type theory. Anywhere else (Agda without its cubical mode, Coq, Lean) one first
-- builds a model of cubical sets (presheaves, Kan structure, Glue, univalence); here the model is
-- the host: object paths are host paths, object composition is the host's composition, Glue and
-- univalence are the host's; meanings are host terms, so soundness holds by construction
-- (reifySound is refl) and only readback needs designing. The method for the Kan operations, Glue
-- and universes is published (Sterling and Angiuli, LICS 2021; Cubical Agda, cooltt). Open, and
-- deciding how much of the remaining work is needed: the host identifies interval expressions up to
-- the De Morgan laws judgmentally, yet F2 re-proves every law as a path, because a context's
-- dimensions are encoded as a cube built by recursion on their number, so at a variable number
-- every operation is a stuck recursion. A representation in which an object dimension is a host
-- interval variable would get those laws for free; how to have one when the number of dimensions is
-- a variable is the question to settle before continuing F2d and F2e. Question for Avik
-- (2026-11-13): what is the meaning of a term in a context with a variable number of dimensions?
-- Tried and not enough: each dimension's value held in the environment as a point of the higher
-- inductive interval (zero, one, seg), path abstraction meaning λ ρ → λ i → ⟦t⟧ (ρ , seg i); meet,
-- join and reversal on its points are definable with judgmental boundaries (imin (seg i) (seg j) =
-- seg (i ∧ j)), but at a variable point x the laws (imin x x = x) are only paths. Until it is
-- answered, experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub (7,477 lines, encoding dimensions as cubes by recursion on
-- their number) is not extended: an answer that changes the encoding replaces most of it. The
-- elaborator's two memory blowups met on the way are below. The longest chain of the project: it
-- should never stop.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub): elaboration: checking a path λ (λ i → sub-
-- pull … keep-S (P i)) whose body applies a defined dependent path P (carry-uniq of a pcat-d)
-- exhausts memory (killed at 5.5 GB; keep-lift's first step, before cong1): the λ's endpoints are
-- computed by evaluating the body at 0 and 1, which unfolds P's definition instead of reading P's
-- endpoints off its type; the same path given to cong1 checks in 2.7M steps. Not yet known: which
-- evaluation step unfolds it. keep-lift's parts: the cubes of contexts its base paths pass through
-- (kl-k2, kl-k3), the meanings over them (kl-E0 … kl-ER0), the dependent paths over each base path
-- (kl-DE, kl-Dr2, kl-D, kl-DR) and the four steps (kl-s1 … kl-s4)
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/std.cub): needs-work. The primitives below give typed programs (the
-- specializer, the translation to machine code) the untyped checker's own operations at types: a
-- definition's code, quotation, normalization, conversion at a type, constructor tables, rewriting.
-- They are trusted, as the checker is, and their types are asserted, not checked. Typed codes
-- (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub, typed-codes) replace them by terms whose types are checked.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- the checker's services, at their
-- types The checker (lib/check.cub) and the code utilities beside it (lib/match.cub) are terms
-- written before types exist. These give typed programs over codes (the specializer, the compiler)
-- the services they read at the types they have. Trusted with the checker (PLAN §7). a checked
-- definition's value quoted at its type (none when it does not quote), and its type's code
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/check.cub): needs-work. The checker is an untyped term with no
-- statement of what it accepts; it is trusted. Paper §6.1: the theorem that it accepts exactly the
-- derivable judgments is separate from its definition. A checker returning a typing derivation
-- (Der) or a refusal is sound by construction; deciding derivability is then its completeness
-- (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub, typed-checker).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/check.cub): needs-work. Solving one implicit argument exhausted the
-- machine's memory (7 GB, killed, exit 137): lib/nbe.cub's `reify-code s K D Δ δ x cx = (fst (fst
-- (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx) , x , embed (snd (fst (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx)))`, where embed's implicit index
-- {KJ} is to be found from its argument's type, at a variable shape s. The same term through a
-- definition with a declared type (nf-code Δ (s , (K , δ , D)) x (fst (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx)) checks
-- in a few thousand steps. Not yet known whether the cost is here (inferring the argument's type by
-- evaluating rr's clauses at the variable s, rather than reading rr's signature) or in the
-- elaborator's syntactic solving. A spine with holes (implicit arguments) is solved where it
-- stands: the head's type is walked along the arguments, a hole standing as a fresh variable, each
-- explicit argument's inferred type matched against its domain, then the result type against the
-- expected one; the universe of a solved type gives its level. The solutions are returned as codes,
-- (elab ...), and the term with them is checked again. An argument's own holes are solved first.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The specializer carries its law in
-- its type, and the three Futamura projections hold (paper §6). paper §6. The specializer
-- normalizes through a primitive that runs the untyped checker, so it cannot be specialized itself;
-- with normalization as a term (typed-codes) it carries its law in its type and the three Futamura
-- projections follow. Specializing a process through a handler was measured superlinear
-- (lib/specialize.cub, 2026-11-09) and is unattributed. It waits on the typed codes above.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/specialize.cub): needs-work. Specialization cost: #build of a process
-- written through a handler was measured superlinear in the number of commands (2026-11-09: 7
-- commands 42M steps, 22 commands 1.21 billion steps, 3 min 39 s; experiments/os/build-cost*.cub
-- hold the per-stage probes). Not attributed (normalization, rewriting, or the passes of
-- lib/x86.cub). It is a cost of the language's own programs about programs.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/specialize.cub): needs-work. Normalization here is code-norm-at, a
-- primitive that runs the evaluator's own quoter (lib/std.cub's checker services). The
-- specializer is therefore not a term all the way down, and cannot be specialized itself: the
-- second and third Futamura projections (paper 6.7) need normalization as a term of the language
-- (normalization by evaluation over typed codes, with its soundness equation 6.5).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Programs are realized as native
-- code through C, and native code replaces a definition only with the relation to its meaning
-- (paper §9). paper §9. Native code today comes from an x86 translator whose output is bound and
-- run with no relation to the definition (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub), from pasted machine code
-- (lib/elf.cub), and with proofs about its bytes at one fixed placement (lib/compile/); nothing
-- relates a fleet's proved behaviour to its binaries (lib/deploy.cub). All of it is replaced by
-- realization through C whose type carries the relation between running the C and the term's
-- meaning. Question for Avik (2026-11-13): that relation needs a meaning of C programs, a C
-- semantics, which is language support, and language support waits on this goal; the paper's §9
-- relates instead to a machine's semantics (a decoded instruction set), and lib/isa/x86 holds an
-- imported x86 model. Is native realization related to the x86 model, C being an unverified
-- convenience, or is a C semantics part of the language implementation?
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): wrong, against the paper (§9.3, "native substitution"): the
-- evaluator may replace a definition by native code only once a target-correctness witness relating
-- the code's execution to the definition exists. Here the bytes are bound after the only check
-- made, that the specialized term converts with the definition (lib/x86.cub jit-plan); the
-- translation from that term to bytes (x-push, x-lift, x-erase, the templates, this boundary's
-- marshaling) carries no relation, so a wrong translation would answer wrongly with nothing to
-- catch it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/deploy.cub): needs-work. fl-beh (what lib/fleet.cub's properties are
-- stated of) and fl-node (the process each binary runs) are both built from cp-view, but nothing
-- relates them: the properties proved of the behaviour are not shown to hold of the binaries. The
-- relation comes from processes realized as executables with their system-call events equal to the
-- process's interaction (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub, native), applied here; nothing specific to
-- this file is owed.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/elf.cub): needs-work. The driver loop, the #build-loop round driver and
-- the start code below are machine code assembled from tools/asm/*.s and pasted as byte literals:
-- trusted as written, with no statement of what they do. They go when processes become executables
-- through C (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub, native).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/compile/meaning.cub): needs-work. This file, lib/compile/runs.cub,
-- lib/compile/tmpl.cub, lib/compile/process.cub's kernel (kn-step, a second kernel beside
-- lib/os/contract.cub's) and experiments/isa/addw-all.cub (one all-inputs theorem, for the bytes of
-- word addition) are proofs about the output of the x86 translator at one fixed placement, a form
-- the paper's §9.3 (Block, seq, the compiler as a fold) replaces. They are to be deleted with that
-- translator; the word, bit and flag law files beside them and x86ok's run lemmas remain
-- usable for x86 as a later target.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every law of the primitives is
-- proved, or named as an assumption (paper §8.5). lib/prim.cub postulates word and bit laws that
-- are provable from the unbounded definitions; fixed-width types have no specification; floating
-- point is trusted without a named assumption (paper §8.5).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/prim.cub): needs-work. Of the 91 primitives here, the laws of word
-- multiplication, division, remainder and the bitwise operations, the wbit-* bit laws, the byte
-- load/store laws, array extensionality and the bit round trips are postulated, though words are
-- unbounded naturals and these laws are provable from the inductive definitions (lib/std.cub
-- already proves addition, equality, order, maximum and subtraction equal to theirs). The fixed-
-- width arithmetic (Int64, UInt32, UInt64) has no specification at all, and floating point is
-- wholly trusted. Paper §8.5: a native primitive implements its definition under a checked theorem
-- or a named assumption. Prove what is provable; define U_w = Fin(2^w) with its modular operations
-- and relate each fixed-width primitive to it or name the assumption; name IEEE binary64 as an
-- assumption. ---------------------------------------------------------------- the types
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Deriving is a typed term over the
-- code of a data declaration. lib/derive.cub's 24 definitions read the checker's own values and are
-- refused as untyped, so `deriving` is off and the Rust readers fail behind it. Deriving becomes a
-- term over the code of a data declaration, which needs a declaration's code as a value (work-as-a-
-- value). It waits on the work being a value (product implementation).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/derive.cub): needs-work. 24 definitions here are untyped and refused by
-- the checker, which has rejected untyped definitions outside the bootstrap files since 2026-11-09,
-- deliberately, after about 10,000 lines of untyped code were written by an agent. They are to be
-- written with types; nothing in the checker changes. `deriving eq` in the surface language depends
-- on this file and is off until it checks. 2026-11-12: they cannot be given types as they are: they
-- read the checker's own values (a constructor's telescope tx A nm r, tval, %var, %view (%gval g),
-- from lib/check.cub, a bootstrap file whose value types are not checked data), which no type of a
-- checked definition names. Typed, deriving is a term over a code of the data type's declaration
-- (its constructors and field types, as Code), not over the checker's values.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The evaluator handles terms of any
-- depth without quadratic cost or stack overflow. measured 2026-11-09, building a term cost
-- quadratic time in its depth and deep terms overflowed the C stack (src/net.c). Measured
-- 2026-11-13 at depths 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000: 2.6 s, 1.5 s, 4.0 s, so no longer quadratic there;
-- the stack at depth 10^6 is not re-measured.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): question. Measured 2026-11-09: build_m calls synkey at every
-- node it builds and synkey walks the whole subterm, so building a term of depth d costs d^2 key
-- computations (a unary numeral of 4*10^4 did not print in 260 s), and deep right-nested terms (a
-- numeral of 10^6) overflow the C stack in several recursive functions of this file and src/lang.c.
-- Not re-measured since; whether both still hold is not known.
language-implementation : Node
language-implementation = node "The Hyperactive language and its features, implemented completely and correctly as the paper specifies." (cons the-product nil)
-- paper-examples (2026-11-13, Avik): "term-by-term construction of every single construction
-- including examples, appendix, in paper, as code in repo cited by content-address in paper ...
-- expressely de-prioritized ... while this *serves nothing*." It serves no node but the root, so
-- the order of work gives it nothing while anything else is open.
paper-examples : Node
paper-examples = node "Every construction in the paper, its examples and appendix included, as a term in the repository, cited from the paper by content address." (cons the-product nil)
-- product-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "all the product work of building a live interactive
-- collaboration system & tooling over the language & its terms". How it is held, changed and shared:
-- HYPERACTIVE.md §2.3.
-- The product is not a system built on the language and has no parts: it is the language's own
-- notions taken seriously, one organism (Avik, 2026-11-13). A definition's identity is its content;
-- so a change is a new term, related to the old by a path or a square in the language; so nothing is
-- overwritten and there is no canonical state to merge into; so a view is a term the language
-- evaluates over the growing set of terms; and the interface is those views rendered for a person.
-- Each follows from the one before; none is built apart from the others.
-- What is known about this goal follows (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): facts,
-- not a breakdown and not parts; the session working on the goal decides how to break it down when its
-- work begins (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4).
-- Known (2026-11-13, Avik's correction): identity is semantic, two terms being the same exactly
-- when their content is; SHA-256 equality is one witness of it, not something interpretation
-- computes. Inside a run the evaluator's sound representation is interning (terms built from equal
-- parts share one node, so pointer equality is content identity, proved once about the interning);
-- a hash is needed only where identity crosses what a pointer cannot (another run, another machine,
-- the store), under collision resistance as a named assumption. So the findings below that ask for
-- every reference to be keyed by its content hash name the wrong fix: what is missing is
-- translating an evaluator result's run-local identity into a portable one when it leaves the run.
-- This is the paper's pattern throughout: a mechanism (native code, a primitive, a pointer, a hash)
-- is admitted only with its relation to the defined meaning.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the change-management reasoning): holding an object's content address
-- cannot be what grants seeing it, because the address of low-entropy content (a short message, a
-- small edit, a number) is guessable by hashing candidates; so visibility is decided where an
-- object crosses a boundary, the same place run-local identity becomes portable identity, and an
-- object whose audience is restricted gets a portable key that only its audience can compute, which
-- gives up sharing it with identical objects outside that audience and changes nothing about its
-- semantic identity.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work leaves git for the change
-- management Avik designed. The design, in his words, is HYPERACTIVE.md §2.3:
-- the work is a collection of immutable objects, not a tree of files (§1); a session reads and
-- writes through a query interface and materializes locally only to act, an executable and its
-- input (§2); what a session does internally stays invisible to Avik's views (§3); there is no
-- canonical state and nothing is promoted, every change being new code (§4, §5); old objects are
-- marked dead by a statement with a reason (§6); a change to an inner construction relates its
-- users' old and new versions by a square (§7); questions are tasks with reasons (§8). Git and
-- Pijul are the wrong base (§5). Until it exists, git carries the work: one session, on
-- main only, no branch, every change pushed the moment it is made (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every definition and every result
-- is identified by its content and kept, so work is shared across runs and collaborators.
-- definitions and results identified by content, the foundation of collaboration with no canonical
-- state (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.3). A reference is keyed by its index in the run
-- (src/net.c), so no result is kept or shared beyond a run (src/lang.c); the checker's identity is
-- a hash of file texts (src/store.c); the store writes in place, so a killed machine leaves a
-- truncated entry (src/store.c); the standard commands never use the store (Makefile). Elaborations
-- are already remembered (a warm library check, 4.6 s). It also makes every check fast, which
-- speeds all other work.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Every evaluator
-- result read back here (checks, the elaborator's solving rounds via %chk-def / %infer-ok, usages,
-- readings) lives only for this run. Each is to be kept beyond it in canonical form: the query
-- identified by its canonical key (references as content keys; the checker identified by its
-- own definitions' keys), the result stored with references as keys and rendered through the
-- current bindings when read, the same mechanism as the store's definitions. The store's
-- certificates are then such results; the per-file cache goes (gone 2026-11-12). Read 2026-11-12:
-- the query built here (build_top) is keyed in src/net.c by synkey, but that key's leaves are run-
-- local: a definition by its global index (key_global), a λ or thunk by the address of its compiled
-- record (K_LAM over t->rec), and the codes passed name checked definitions by their index (cglob
-- i). So the evaluator's key of a query means nothing in another run. Two key schemes exist: the
-- evaluator's (structural, run-local) and the store's (canonical forms, content keys). The end
-- state is one: every key canonical, compiled records keyed by their content. The first step
-- can be taken here: the query's key formed as the store forms a definition's (its canonical form,
-- references as content keys, plus the checker's identity), and the result stored canonically.
-- 2026-11-12: one kind of result is kept so: a declaration's whole elaboration (src/syntax.c,
-- x_checked; below, done). The evaluator's own results, %chk-def's and the readings', are not yet.
-- Read 2026-11-12, for the elaborator's case (src/syntax.c x_checked, the round loop): the right
-- unit is the whole elaboration of a declaration, its input the declaration's surface forms (sig,
-- body) with every name resolved to the key it denotes, its output the solved core forms T and t.
-- The final forms then go through `checked` as now, so the certificate check (key_def,
-- store_checked) still decides acceptance: a remembered elaboration can only change which term is
-- checked, never whether a term is accepted. What is missing is a faithful canonical serialization:
-- canon (src/ store.c) is a key, not a serialization. It drops the type written on a λ's binder
-- (bnd_ty, kept on the name node), instance flags and binder names, and prints a λ's binders so
-- their count cannot be read back. The store needs one canonical form that is both the key and
-- readable back (references as keys, binders nameless but counted, binder types and flags kept),
-- rendered through the current bindings when read; with it canon is that form's hash and nothing is
-- duplicated. Done 2026-11-12 (branch product-implementation, merged): canon's binders readable
-- back and decanon (every library definition round-trips); generated names numbered by first
-- occurrence in an elaboration's key and in its stored form, binders' kept names included (x-case-
-- chain had failed warm, "rec of a name that is not a recursive field of its branch", its pattern's
-- field renumbered while the rebuilt scrutinee's let kept the raw name that `rec` resolves); data
-- keys nominal; the elaboration remembered. Acceptance is not remembered with it: a
-- remembered elaboration is a term like any other, accepted by its own key's certificate or by the
-- checker (the first version marked a hit as already checked, which would have accepted a stale
-- elaboration unchecked). Measured, the library with the store warm and the per-file cache cold:
-- 4.2 s of CPU, no evaluator step, the same 1907 passed and 52 failed as cold (29 s before).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). A reference to a
-- definition is keyed by its index in the current run, so no key involving one means the same thing
-- in another run, and no evaluator result mentioning a definition can be remembered beyond the run.
-- The key of a reference is to be the definition's content key (src/store.c's key of its canonical
-- form); equal content is equal meaning, so this is sound for values. Results that name definitions
-- (readback text, elaboration solutions) are then stored canonically and rendered through current
-- bindings. 2026-11-12: a λ or thunk is likewise keyed by the address of its compiled record
-- (synkey, K_LAM over t->rec), so compiled records need content keys too for any key to be
-- canonical.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/store.c): the checker's identity here is the hash of the texts of the
-- files loaded up to the checker's definition (comments and unrelated definitions included) plus
-- the machine binary, not the content keys of the definitions the checker consists of. So a comment
-- edited in the bootstrap files invalidates every certificate in the store.
-- Known (2026-11-13, at src/store.c): wrong. store_write writes an entry in place (fopen "w"). A
-- machine killed while writing (measured 2026-11-13: the kernel killed one for lack of memory while
-- four read in parallel) leaves a truncated entry that later runs read as a whole one, and two
-- machines on one store can read each other's half-written entries; the store is a cache whose
-- entries decide what is not checked again, so a truncated entry could change a result. Fix: write
-- to a temporary file in the same directory and rename it over the entry (atomic on POSIX). Until
-- then tools/task- graph.py gives each parallel reader its own store.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at Makefile): every target sets CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1, so the standard commands
-- never use the store and recheck everything from nothing (64 s for the library). With the store
-- warm, elaboration and compilation still run for every definition (29 s), so incremental checking
-- reaches only the checker's run, not the rest of the work done per definition. Measured again
-- 2026-11-12, the elaboration and the checker's answers (accepted or refused) kept in the store,
-- the per-file cache gone: with the store warm the library takes 4.6 s, its output the fresh run's
-- but for step counts; what remains per run is compilation (compiled records keyed by content,
--). The targets still check from nothing.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work is a value of the
-- language: every declaration's code and reason readable by views, and the interface reads it.
-- serve mode gives views every declaration of a type as a value, but not each declaration's code
-- (src/lang.c); most declarations have no recorded reason (lib/task- graph.cub). The interface for
-- Avik (no editor, no IDE) reads the work through it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Serve mode is how
-- agents and people use the work continuously. Missing: the work given to a term as a value (the
-- declarations and their bindings, each declaration's code), so that views are terms of the
-- language applied to it, and a reading that evaluates a view. Replacing a binding is #retract then
-- a new declaration. 2026-11-12: `declarations T` is every checked definition of type
-- T in scope, as a list (src/ syntax.c), so views over values of a type are terms (lib/task-
-- graph.cub's over Node) and `#show` of one is the reading that evaluates it (tools/task-graph.py
-- view). Still missing: each declaration's code as a value, so that a view can read the code
-- itself, every declaration of every type.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/task-graph.cub): The work as one graph of nodes. Done: goals and
-- tickets are one kind of node (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub); every declaration of the code is a
-- node of the same graph, its reason computed from the machine's reading `#uses` (a declaration
-- with a reason mentions it), from the machine naming it (src/), or stated in the comment above it
-- as `(part of NODE)` or `(prerequisite to NODE)` (tools/task-graph.py why, unexplained). Measured
-- 2026-11-12 over every file: 4310 of 8312 declarations have no reason; stating the rest where the
-- code's text establishes a reason is what remains (HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4). Library
-- API nothing uses (lib/std.cub's pow, fact, happly, the univalence lemmas; lib/prelude.cub's
-- untyped helpers) states none: whether each stays is open.
product-implementation : Node
product-implementation = node "The live interactive collaboration system and tooling over the language and its terms." (cons the-product nil)
-- language-support (2026-11-13, Avik): "Languages support (semantics/syntax as terms for particular
-- languages) * RISC x86, C++, C, Rust, OCaml, Python etc". "before we start any such work, certain
-- things need to be at perfection": it waits on language-implementation and product-implementation
-- (after, below).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at experiments/isa/build-run.cub): needs-work. The imported x86 model has no
-- translation of the two-byte opcode 0F AE (mfence and its group), an inventory gap of the Sail
-- import; any binary using it stops there under the model.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/isa/tso.cub): question. TsoSound is stated and has no inhabitant: no
-- definition proves it. The litmus files in experiments/tso establish instances by computation for
-- particular threads and bounded schedules only. Likewise AtomsX86Correct in lib/mem/rc11.cub.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. The C++ reading has drifted from what it
-- stands on, measured 2026-11-12 with each file loaded alone after its imports (--read): here 21
-- failures (type mismatches at lines 64 and 78 and others; a primitive declared outside the
-- bootstrap files, refused); lib/cxx/read-expr.cub 70 (its cases miss constructors lib/cxx/ast.cub
-- gained later: ce-string- literal-units, ck-FloatingCast, clayout-v, ctk-preferred-alignof);
-- experiments/mem/sum.cub (a record field mem-allocs and ev-run-pure, ev-value that no longer
-- exist). The renaming of lib/mem/scalar.cub's operators to ao-... had not reached these files or
-- lib/lang/rust/read- rvalue.cub; done 2026-11-12. The readers are to be brought to the AST and the
-- memory as they are.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. Measured 2026-11-11 (memory files, then this
-- file and read-expr.cub, after the library): this file 21 failures, read-expr.cub 70. Roots: cx-
-- unsupported (here) and cx-run (below) do not check against CxEv's type; the primitive cx-byte is
-- refused (primitives exist only in the bootstrap files); the rest follow from these (cx-sk, cx-
-- size undefined; implicit arguments applied to functions without them). The C++ reading does not
-- check as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/read-expr.cub): wrong. This file refers to read.cub (statements,
-- declarations, the fuel that ties calls) for meanings it does not define; no lib/cxx/read.cub
-- exists. The C++ reading is incomplete as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/lang/rust/machine.cub): needs-work. Measured 2026-11-11: these deriving
-- lines fail because lib/derive.cub, which deriving needs, is refused as untyped; the Rust
-- readers then fail on what follows (machine 6, read-place 21, read-rvalue 26, read 22 failures).
-- Typing derive.cub repairs the root.
language-support : Node
language-support = node "The syntax and semantics of particular languages as terms: x86, C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and others." (cons the-product nil)
-- documents that are essential context for working on a node and everything below it
-- (lib/task-graph.cub; HYPERACTIVE.md §2.4)
hyperactive-design : Document
hyperactive-design = document "../HYPERACTIVE.md"
hyperactive-paper : Document
hyperactive-paper = document "../docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf"
-- the files each task concerns (2026-11-13)
file-experiments-isa-build-run-cub : Document
file-experiments-isa-build-run-cub = document "../experiments/isa/build-run.cub"
file-experiments-nbe-p26-wip-cub : Document
file-experiments-nbe-p26-wip-cub = document "../experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub"
file-lib-check-cub : Document
file-lib-check-cub = document "../lib/check.cub"
file-lib-compile-meaning-cub : Document
file-lib-compile-meaning-cub = document "../lib/compile/meaning.cub"
file-lib-cxx-machine-cub : Document
file-lib-cxx-machine-cub = document "../lib/cxx/machine.cub"
file-lib-cxx-read-expr-cub : Document
file-lib-cxx-read-expr-cub = document "../lib/cxx/read-expr.cub"
file-lib-deploy-cub : Document
file-lib-deploy-cub = document "../lib/deploy.cub"
file-lib-derive-cub : Document
file-lib-derive-cub = document "../lib/derive.cub"
file-lib-elf-cub : Document
file-lib-elf-cub = document "../lib/elf.cub"
file-lib-isa-tso-cub : Document
file-lib-isa-tso-cub = document "../lib/isa/tso.cub"
file-lib-lang-rust-machine-cub : Document
file-lib-lang-rust-machine-cub = document "../lib/lang/rust/machine.cub"
file-lib-nbe-cub : Document
file-lib-nbe-cub = document "../lib/nbe.cub"
file-lib-prelude-cub : Document
file-lib-prelude-cub = document "../lib/prelude.cub"
file-lib-prim-cub : Document
file-lib-prim-cub = document "../lib/prim.cub"
file-lib-specialize-cub : Document
file-lib-specialize-cub = document "../lib/specialize.cub"
file-lib-std-cub : Document
file-lib-std-cub = document "../lib/std.cub"
file-lib-task-graph-cub : Document
file-lib-task-graph-cub = document "../lib/task-graph.cub"
file-makefile : Document
file-makefile = document "../Makefile"
file-src-lang-c : Document
file-src-lang-c = document "../src/lang.c"
file-src-net-c : Document
file-src-net-c = document "../src/net.c"
file-src-store-c : Document
file-src-store-c = document "../src/store.c"
essential-context : List (Node × List Document)
essential-context = cons (the-product , cons hyperactive-design (cons hyperactive-paper nil)) (cons (product-implementation , cons file-src-lang-c (cons file-src-net-c (cons file-src-store-c (cons file-makefile (cons file-lib-task-graph-cub nil))))) (cons (language-implementation , cons file-src-lang-c (cons file-src-net-c (cons file-lib-check-cub (cons file-lib-prelude-cub (cons file-lib-deploy-cub (cons file-lib-elf-cub (cons file-lib-compile-meaning-cub (cons file-experiments-nbe-p26-wip-cub (cons file-lib-std-cub (cons file-lib-nbe-cub (cons file-lib-derive-cub (cons file-lib-prim-cub (cons file-lib-specialize-cub nil))))))))))))) (cons (language-support , cons file-experiments-isa-build-run-cub (cons file-lib-isa-tso-cub (cons file-lib-cxx-machine-cub (cons file-lib-cxx-read-expr-cub (cons file-lib-lang-rust-machine-cub nil))))) nil)))
-- the root: what Avik wants
roots : List Node
roots = cons the-product nil
-- language support starts only when the language implementation and the product implementation are
-- done (Avik, 2026-11-13)
after : List (Node × List Node)
after = cons (language-support , cons language-implementation (cons product-implementation nil)) nil
